= Contradiction — Simple =

A '''contradiction''' happens when two claims cannot both be true in the way they are being used.

A simple logical form is:

<pre>
P
not P
</pre>

For example, “the switch is on now” and “the switch is not on now” contradict each other if they refer to the same switch and same time.

Disagreement is not always contradiction. Two claims may sound opposed but actually refer to different times, meanings, or situations.

In classical logic, a contradiction inside a theory creates serious problems because arbitrary conclusions can be derived from an inconsistency.

